WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.151
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.451 --> 00:00:10.070
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:17.550
Unit twenty-one: Science Journalism. Today you will state likelihood with be likely to, bound to and unlikely to.

4
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.259
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.444
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:26.702
Science journalism.

7
00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:32.270
Science journalism means reporting on scientific research for the general public.

8
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:36.473
Science journalism turns difficult papers into clear stories.

9
00:00:37.083 --> 00:00:40.293
Your turn. Say: Science journalism.

10
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:45.121
Science journalism.

11
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:46.861
Research.

12
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.173
Research means careful study to discover new facts.

13
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:53.690
The research took five years.

14
00:00:54.283 --> 00:00:57.481
Your turn. Say: Research.

15
00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:01.195
Research.

16
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:02.951
Evidence.

17
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:07.261
Evidence means facts or signs that show something is true.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:09.931
The evidence supports the new theory.

19
00:01:10.517 --> 00:01:13.146
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

20
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.071
Evidence.

21
00:01:17.817 --> 00:01:18.992
Uncertainty.

22
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:23.608
Uncertainty means a state in which something is not known for sure.

23
00:01:24.217 --> 00:01:26.820
Honest reports explain the uncertainty.

24
00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:30.185
Your turn. Say: Uncertainty.

25
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.360
Uncertainty.

26
00:01:35.117 --> 00:01:36.153
Explain.

27
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.659
Explain means to make an idea clear and easy to understand.

28
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.016
Journalists explain complex results simply.

29
00:01:44.617 --> 00:01:47.432
Your turn. Say: Explain.

30
00:01:50.267 --> 00:01:51.384
Explain.

31
00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.047
Expert.

32
00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:57.013
Expert means a person with deep knowledge of a subject.

33
00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.883
An expert checked the claims.

34
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:02.996
Your turn. Say: Expert.

35
00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:06.800
Expert.

36
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:08.760
Accuracy.

37
00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.620
Accuracy means being correct in every detail.

38
00:02:13.217 --> 00:02:15.367
Accuracy builds readers' trust.

39
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:18.531
Your turn. Say: Accuracy.

40
00:02:21.367 --> 00:02:22.461
Accuracy.

41
00:02:23.217 --> 00:02:24.601
Public understanding.

42
00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:29.474
Public understanding means how well ordinary people grasp a topic.

43
00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:32.977
Public understanding of vaccines has improved.

44
00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:36.735
Your turn. Say: Public understanding.

45
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.042
Public understanding.

46
00:02:41.833 --> 00:02:45.473
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:46.317 --> 00:02:53.555
Visal, a news story says 'The treatment is likely to help most patients' and 'The drug is unlikely to cause harm.' What is the structure?

48
00:02:54.650 --> 00:03:05.653
Be likely / unlikely / bound / certain / sure to + base verb. It states probability. Likely is probable; bound means almost certain; unlikely means improbable.

49
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.993
Why is it important in science news?

50
00:03:10.083 --> 00:03:21.606
Because researchers rarely know for sure. A careful journalist chooses the right level: 'may' is weak, 'is likely to' is stronger, 'is certain to' should be rare.

51
00:03:22.717 --> 00:03:24.229
Any other options?

52
00:03:25.317 --> 00:03:38.697
It is likely that… or There is a good chance that… Never write 'will definitely' about a single study. Use the perfect infinitive for the past: The study is likely to have been affected by sampling.

53
00:03:39.833 --> 00:03:41.252
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:42.117 --> 00:03:48.180
Grammar focus: Probability: be likely and unlikely and bound and certain to plus verb.

55
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:49.690
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:50.383 --> 00:03:52.824
The treatment is likely to help most patients.

57
00:03:53.717 --> 00:03:56.250
The drug is unlikely to cause serious harm.

58
00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:00.520
The study is likely to have been affected by the small sample.

59
00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:08.763
Use be likely / unlikely / bound / certain to + base verb for probability. Choose the strength of the word to match the strength of the evidence.

60
00:04:09.517 --> 00:04:13.423
Be careful! Don't say: The drug is unlikely cause harm.

61
00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:18.086
Say: The drug is unlikely to cause harm.

62
00:04:18.386 --> 00:04:21.140
Use to + base verb after unlikely.

63
00:04:21.933 --> 00:04:24.574
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:26.704
Reporting with Care.

65
00:04:27.004 --> 00:04:31.303
Science journalism explains new research to readers who are not experts.

66
00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.296
A careful writer is unlikely to promise a cure after one small study.

67
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:41.622
An expert will often say that the evidence is likely to change as more data arrive.

68
00:04:42.633 --> 00:04:48.871
Accuracy means describing uncertainty honestly, because readers are bound to trust a clear and fair report.

69
00:04:49.867 --> 00:04:55.570
Public understanding improves when journalists explain the limits of the research as well as the results.

70
00:04:56.667 --> 00:05:00.692
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:03.805
The results blank change with more data.

72
00:05:04.105 --> 00:05:07.770
A, are likely to. B, likely. C, are likely.

73
00:05:11.333 --> 00:05:13.379
The answer is A: are likely to.

74
00:05:14.517 --> 00:05:17.515
Facts or signs that show something is true are blank.

75
00:05:17.815 --> 00:05:21.967
A, uncertainty. B, evidence. C, headlines.

76
00:05:25.533 --> 00:05:27.858
The answer is B: evidence.

77
00:05:29.017 --> 00:05:30.633
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:30.933 --> 00:05:37.895
A, The drug is unlikely cause harm.. B, The drug is unlikely to cause harm.. C, The drug unlikely is to cause harm..

79
00:05:41.433 --> 00:05:44.907
The answer is B: The drug is unlikely to cause harm..

80
00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:48.549
Being correct in every detail is blank.

81
00:05:48.849 --> 00:05:52.595
A, accuracy. B, opinion. C, debate.

82
00:05:56.133 --> 00:05:58.028
The answer is A: accuracy.

83
00:05:59.183 --> 00:06:03.203
According to the reading, what does one small study rarely justify?

84
00:06:03.503 --> 00:06:08.341
A, promising a cure. B, writing a title. C, asking an expert.

85
00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:14.410
The answer is A: promising a cure.

86
00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.833
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:19.683 --> 00:06:23.343
Why do science headlines sometimes exaggerate findings?

88
00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:33.708
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:34.008 --> 00:06:59.480
Science headlines sometimes exaggerate because editors want attention, and strong words attract clicks. A careful study may say a result is likely, while a headline says it is proven. Uncertainty is harder to put in a few words. Readers should therefore look at the full article and the evidence, and journalists should choose accuracy over excitement.

90
00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.489
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:02.383 --> 00:07:09.900
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and probability: be likely and unlikely and bound and certain to plus verb.

92
00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:15.604
Today's value is intellectual honesty. Follow the evidence, even when it is inconvenient.

93
00:07:15.904 --> 00:07:18.580
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.335
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

